# Use Context7 MCP for Library Docs

When you need to see **any documentation** (libraries, APIs, frameworks, packages), use the **Context7** MCP server to retrieve up-to-date docs and code examples instead of relying only on built-in knowledge or web search.

## Scope boundary

- Use this rule for **external** documentation (libraries/frameworks/APIs).
- For **internal project docs** (`README.md`, `CONTRACTS.md`, `docs/**`, `CherryPlayList/docs/**`), follow the project documentation rule first.

## When to use

- Looking up library/framework API (React, Vue, TypeScript, Vite, etc.)
- Checking package usage, options, or examples
- Verifying current docs for a dependency
- Need official or up-to-date code samples

## How to use

1. **Resolve library ID:** Call `resolve-library-id` (or equivalent) with the library/package name and a short query describing what you need. This returns one or more Context7-compatible library IDs (e.g. `/websites/cursor`, `/vercel/next.js`).
2. **Query documentation:** Call `query-docs` with the chosen `libraryId` and a specific question or topic. Use the ID from step 1; do not call `query-docs` without a valid library ID unless the user explicitly provided one in the format `/org/project` or `/org/project/version`.
3. Prefer Context7 over guessing from memory when accuracy or recency matters.

## Note

"Context7" in project discussions refers to this documentation MCP. Always prefer it when the task involves reading or following official documentation.
